sections.content:
The try statement allows you to define a block of code to be 
tested for errors while it is being executed.

The catch statement allows you to define a block of code to 
be executed, if an error occurs in the try block.

The JavaScript statements try and catch 
come in pairs:

try {

   Block of code to try

} catch(err) {

   Block of code to handle errors

}

A ReferenceError occurs if you use (reference) a variable that does not exist.

Error TypeExampleError

ReferenceErrorfname = foo;
foo is not defined

ReferenceErrorlet x = y;
let y = 5;
Cannot access y before initialization

Examples
You cannot use a non-existing variable:

let x = 5;

try {

  x = y + 1;

} catch(err) {

  let text = err.name;

}

Try it Yourself »
Cannot access a variable before initialization:

try {

  let x = y;

