If a component takes time to load, you can use a Suspense component,
and it will display the fallback content while the component is loading.

Example
The Fruits component takes two seconds to load,
so we wrap it in a Suspense component to display a loading message while it is loading.

import { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client';
import { Suspense } from 'react';
import Fruits from './Fruits';

function App() {
 return (
 <div>
 <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
 <Fruits />
 </Suspense>
 </div>
 );
}

createRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(
 <App />
);

Run Example »

Another common use of the Suspense component is when importing components with lazy loading:

In the example above we had to fake a delay of two seconds to see the loading message. A task like displaying three fruits from an array
 would be too fast to see the loading message at all.

But with lazy loading, we can import a component dynamically, and it will display a loading message while it is loading,
 even if the task is very fast.

Lets first create an example WITHOUT using lazy loading, where we do not fake a two seconds delay:

Example
This example is too fast to see the loading message:

import { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client';
import { Suspense } from 'react';
import Cars from './Cars';

function App() {
 return (
 <div>
 <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
 <Cars />
 </Suspense>
 </div>
 );
}

createRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(
 <App />
);

Run Example »

Now let us create an example WITH using lazy loading:

Example
Same example as above, but using lazy loading:

import { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client';
import { Suspense, lazy } from 'react';

const Cars = lazy(() => import('./Cars'));

function App() {
 return (
 <div>
 <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
 <Cars />
 </Suspense>
 </div>
 );
}

createRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(
 <App />
);

Run Example »

 Example Explained

 lazy() lets you load a component dynamically

 Suspense shows a fallback while the component loads

